  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B034",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks and their exceptional precision in timekeeping"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B020",
    "B003"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I retain B034 as my preferred configuration. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea brought exquisite control of atomic quantum systems together with a decisive metrological advance. Katori's originating concept and Ye's defining experimental development form a clear, balanced attribution. Most importantly, this is no longer a promising technique: independent laboratories have attained extraordinary performance, so its physical basis and precision are thoroughly tested.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that major prospective consequences, including future metrological redefinition and some fundamental-physics applications, remain developing. We should therefore frame the award around the demonstrated clock architecture and precision achievement, rather than anticipated applications.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll stresses that Katori originated the magic-wavelength concept, Ye extended its precision-measurement reach, and independent laboratories have achieved extraordinary performance.",
        "response": "I agree with this assessment. It reinforces that the prize-worthy achievement is a distinct physical architecture with reproducible performance, not merely an incremental improvement in clocks. I would retain a concise citation so that later applications do not blur that central discovery."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that Cirac and Zoller provided a concrete, influential atomic-physics route to quantum computation and simulation, repeatedly realized in ion and neutral-atom platforms.",
        "response": "B020 remains an exceptionally strong alternative, particularly from the standpoint of atomic physics and the unusually clear joint theoretical contribution. I nevertheless place B034 ahead because lattice clocks already offer a more sharply delimited achievement whose extraordinary precision has been replicated as a mature measurement capability; the boundary between the Cirac-Zoller proposals and the wider quantum-computing enterprise needs more careful delimitation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson considers quantum error correction foundational and extensively validated, while noting that practical scale and dominant hardware implementations remain unsettled.",
        "response": "I share the view that B003 identifies a profound conceptual solution to decoherence, and it is a serious alternative. The unresolved demonstration of full fault tolerance, however, matters for maturity. It makes the fully established, independently reproducible physical achievement of B034 the sounder choice at present."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the credited pair fully captures the original conceptual and experimental lineage of optical lattice clocks.",
      "How narrowly the citation should distinguish demonstrated clock physics from future metrological and fundamental-physics uses."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
